{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"In-kind creations/redemptions require assets that can actually move between the ETF and an authorized participant (AP). If derivative positions cannot practicably transfer under the dealing arrangements, the AP may instead deliver or receive **cash**, either for the whole basket or as a cash component. The ETF then establishes, adjusts, or unwinds exposure through its counterparties or brokers as needed; redemption cash may also come from available liquidity.\n\nBy contrast, transferable securities can be delivered directly in-kind, potentially avoiding fund-level trading and improving tax efficiency.\n\n**Limits:** This is an operational explanation, not a blanket rule requiring derivatives ETFs to deal in cash. Some derivative positions can transfer; securities baskets can also use cash for other reasons. Actual arrangements depend on fund documents, counterparty/clearing arrangements, and applicable rules.\n\n**Worth knowing:** Cash dealing can introduce trading costs, spreads, market impact, and potential tax consequences; fees may allocate some costs to transacting APs."}}
